#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096893 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "977a9b6e-744a-554b-92ee-0b6343041ecf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of the Second Derivative of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9506c823-cef4-5d91-9257-e951da7e14b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093766} {:answer/id #uuid "96f522bf-2e6b-5264-b5d1-c9aec4b72f15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093767} {:answer/id #uuid "ab3d74cb-4051-57ed-87b3-5aa7fa3e3d1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g(x)$ has no inflection points" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093768} {:answer/id #uuid "bf78219d-1db5-5974-98fa-1e76ec4c3d18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = ± 1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093769} {:answer/id #uuid "ce8be3f5-76b3-577c-96fa-dd6e0441f402" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093770}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab3d74cb-4051-57ed-87b3-5aa7fa3e3d1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g(x)$ has no inflection points" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093768} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9d5cd05-b8b3-5204-84c4-7e0767455567" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093765}] :question/id #uuid "47a20fe2-66d7-5e4d-88bc-ebcb0a87e290" :question/math-academy-id "q-114418" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1046/Source/Images/q-114418.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x$. The graph $y = g^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $g(x)$ have a point of inflection?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306050 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a363f215-ed40-504a-bcae-620b7d585678" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points of Inflection Using Differentiation When Some Points Must Be Discarded"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "adef789f-225b-5894-a8b9-5e11934c377c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306052} {:answer/id #uuid "ee1fd109-16af-5879-bcfd-4d1366864d1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306053} {:answer/id #uuid "0cd4ab4f-95a1-5b0c-80a0-5c09c687f7aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306054} {:answer/id #uuid "abf597b9-e766-597c-b9d7-6faf4a80df03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no points of inflection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306055} {:answer/id #uuid "6d418e2c-fc04-5c80-b390-b1fd8c7464a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306056}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abf597b9-e766-597c-b9d7-6faf4a80df03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no points of inflection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306055} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc031990-69d2-56b3-825b-b891ab692887" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306051}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "80fd1b01-b4c3-5a42-879f-a9a61102a17e" :question/math-academy-id "q-21035" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=12x-7+\\frac{3}{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =12-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}}=0\n$$\n\nThe equation above has no real solutions, so there are no points of inflection."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294077 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "977a9b6e-744a-554b-92ee-0b6343041ecf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of the Second Derivative of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dc8b157-7c31-5520-86d5-398490e4cf98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294079} {:answer/id #uuid "c2b09eeb-837b-5d5d-8fc4-0f380227af07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294080} {:answer/id #uuid "62d0cf2f-a834-5fcc-ac44-2090d7ab89f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294081} {:answer/id #uuid "13fb1a98-3b74-5fc9-94b5-677f6f9553db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3,$ $x=0,$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294082} {:answer/id #uuid "48c2fa81-7f35-5e0a-955d-625d74477f8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294083}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62d0cf2f-a834-5fcc-ac44-2090d7ab89f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294081} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7a6ffea-ff76-537a-8399-e287f6d1cecc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1fbbf821-90cb-5820-99c9-f84523b8d2c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-79200" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/assets/012c84c1dfaeb5802adbecea30c3c184a13b6802f2509e5983f6b3682fe39f79.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x)$ must switch signs. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x=-3$ and $x=4.$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=-3$ and $x=4.$"}]}
